        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Growth, existing neighborhoods, and new settlements",
        "content": "The framework accommodates both expansion and work within existing urban fabric. It classifies neighborhood improvement, renewal, reconstruction, and restoration schemes as forms of detailed planning. Land preparation for new urban development is another recognized application. New towns follow a separate establishment process, after which master and detailed plans are also required. These distinctions help explain why regeneration, new-town development, and land-use management belong to the same field while addressing different starting conditions. [s1]\n\nThe history of this system should also be read with care. Elmira Jafari's research on Tehran examines exchanges between Iranian and foreign planners and challenges accounts that describe modern planning simply as an imported Western project. Her published abstract emphasizes changing professional roles and the relationship between planning ideas, policies, and urban consequences. Tehran provides an important historical case, but it cannot establish how every Iranian city developed. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "implementation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Approval and implementation are different questions",
        "content": "A published systematic review of Iranian urban-development research identifies overlapping responsibilities, organizational conflict, legal complexity, and unstable funding as obstacles to implementation. These are the review authors' findings, rather than measurements of every municipality. They nevertheless establish why the existence of an approved document cannot alone demonstrate that its proposals were delivered. [s3]\n\nFor readers comparing cities, the useful questions are concrete: which document was approved, what area does it cover, which body is responsible for each intervention, and what evidence shows implementation? A plan can explain an intended urban structure; assessing the resulting city requires additional evidence about decisions, investment, and completed work. This distinction follows from the implementation problems identified in the review. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
