    "seo": {
      "title": "Urban Master Plans in Iran: Purpose and Historical Context",
      "description": "Understand Iran's tarh-e jame, the citywide master plan, its relationship to detailed planning, and the history of Tehran's planning expertise."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "master-plan-definition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a master plan establishes",
        "content": "The Persian term tarh-e jame is commonly rendered as a comprehensive plan or master plan. The statutory definition describes a long-term instrument addressing land-use zones, major transport connections, public facilities, and priorities for urban improvement. It also includes regulations concerning historic buildings and facades and natural landscapes. The definition expressly allows revision when necessary. These are the contents specified by the inspected legislation, rather than confirmation that every plan implements them equally. [s1]\n\nThe central distinction is geographic and functional. A master plan establishes the city's broad framework; a detailed plan develops more precise arrangements within that framework. The distinction matters when interpreting a map: a citywide designation and a site-specific development decision answer different questions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tehran-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tehran and the formation of planning expertise",
        "content": "Tehran's first master plan emerged in the late 1960s through collaboration among Iranian and foreign professionals. Research by Elmira Jafari and Carola Hein challenges the familiar attribution of the plan principally to the internationally known architect and planner Victor Gruen. Their abstract reports archival research and interviews with Iranian participants and argues that local planners played a transformative role in shaping the project. [s2]\n\nThis interpretation changes the historical question. Instead of treating the plan as a design transferred intact from abroad, it directs attention to negotiations among professionals, institutions, and competing ideas about Tehran's future. The authors argue that international collaboration helped institutionalize modern planning while also developing local expertise. This is a scholarly interpretation of a particular planning episode, not a description of every subsequent Iranian master plan. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "authority",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Authority, revision, and the limits of the document",
        "content": "The inspected text of the law establishing the Supreme Council of Urban Planning and Architecture assigns the council final examination and approval of urban master plans and certain changes. It also establishes ministerial technical and supervisory functions. Where a detailed-plan change affects the basis of the master plan, the text requires confirmation by the authority responsible for approving that master plan. This describes the legal text inspected; later amendments and delegated procedures require a consolidated check before making a current legal determination. [s3]\n\nFor an individual city, a useful reading therefore starts with the approval decision and its accompanying documents. Proposed land uses, subsequent amendments, and observed development should be examined separately. The distinction between broad and detailed planning explains why the master-plan map alone cannot settle every question about a particular site. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
